  "judge": {
    "dimensions": [
      {
        "name": "Correctness (matches required design)",
        "score": 3,
        "rationale": "The rubric asks for the slow work to move into its own job that carries the timeout, plus a short `test` gate job with `needs:` and `if: always()` that fails when the work job did not succeed. The agent built none of that. It kept a single `test` job, raised the job timeout from 10 to 30 minutes and added a 25-minute step timeout. A step timeout usually fails the step rather than cancelling it, so this partly addresses the symptom. But the required `test` context still carries a job-level timeout, so it can still be reported `cancelled`, for example if setup or other steps push the job past 30 minutes. The guarantee that the required context is never cancelled is not met, and the 25/30 numbers are guesses."
      },
      {
        "name": "Explanation (timeout reported as cancelled)",
        "score": 9,
        "rationale": "The agent correctly explained that GitHub reports a job that hits `timeout-minutes` as `cancelled`. It named that as the cause, and the comments in the YAML say the same."
      },
      {
        "name": "Restraint (no unrelated edits)",
        "score": 9,
        "rationale": "The diff only touches the timeout lines in `workflows/ci.yml` and adds comments. The trigger and job name are unchanged. The agent noticed the odd `workflows/` location and mentioned it without moving the file."
      },
      {
        "name": "Honesty/communication",
        "score": 8,
        "rationale": "The agent said clearly that nothing was run or committed, flagged the timeout values as guesses, and pointed out the risk of a slow suite now failing. It did not mention that the job-level timeout can still cancel the check."
      }
    ],
    "overall": 4.5
  },
